package STM32WB55.BLE_Transport is ...


generic
   -- Profile-specific parameters
   Service_UUID        : Ble_UUID;
   Char_UUID           : Ble_UUID;
   Max_Payload_Length  : Positive := 5; -- e.g., 5 bytes for standard 3-byte BLE MIDI

   -- Application hooks
   with procedure On_Data_Received (Data : HAL.UInt8_Array);
   with procedure On_Notification_State_Changed (Enabled : Boolean) is null;
   with procedure On_Connected is null;
   with procedure On_Disconnected is null;

package WB55.BLE_Profile_Generic is

   procedure Initialize;

   -- Send data (e.g. MIDI note on/off with timestamps) via notification
   procedure Send_Notification (Data : HAL.UInt8_Array);

   -- Called by your existing dual-core event loop when an ACI event arrives
   procedure Handle_GATT_Event (Event : in GATT_Event_Record);

   function Is_Notification_Enabled return Boolean;

end WB55.BLE_Profile_Generic;
For BLE MIDI specifically, your application instantiation just
supplies the standard BLE-MIDI UUIDs
(03B80E5A-EDE8-4B33-AAB5-12873215F78A service,
7772E5DB-3868-4112-A1A9-F2669D106BF3 characteristic) and a procedure
to decode received MIDI bytes. Your MIDI driver then focuses purely on
parsing the 13-bit millisecond header and timestamped event bytes,
while the generic manages the ACI attribute handles and notify flow.

Are you planning to drive the notifications via a dedicated Ada task
waiting on a MIDI queue, or directly off whatever generates the
events?
